Priya: Quick sync — we're seeing sporadic partial-JSON parse errors in the Go SDK when streaming via SSE. Usually after a brief backpressure spike a downstream consumer sees an "Invalid JSON" and we drop the event. Anyone seen this pattern?
Alex: Yup, common when the server emits an object split across chunks. Are you using NDJSON (newline-delimited) or raw JSON objects framed by the SSE `data:` lines?
Priya: It's `data:` framed ndjson-ish. Example stream fragment we saw: `data: {"id":1,"text":"hello` then next chunk ` world"}

`. Go's decoder errors with EOF on an attempt to decode the first chunk.
Jamal: That sounds like trying to decode partial bytes. In Go we usually buffer until a full record boundary (\n) or use an incremental decoder that can handle streaming. Are you reading by line or by bytes?
Priya: We're reading bytes off net.Conn and splitting on the blank line that separates SSE events — but under backpressure the blank-line can be delayed and we attempt to decode intermediate `data:` payloads prematurely.
Mei: Two practical mitigations: 1) keep assembling SSE event bodies until you see the event terminator (`\n\n`) before handing to JSON decode, 2) use an incremental parser that tolerates partial input (try/catch decode attempts).
Alex: For Go specifically: use a bytes.Buffer and try json.Decoder with `Decode` in a loop, but only after you've concatenated all `data:` lines from the SSE event. Example pattern: buffer the SSE event, then `err := json.NewDecoder(bytes.NewReader(buf.Bytes())).Decode(&obj)` — if you get io.EOF or unexpected EOF, keep buffering, but protect with a size/timeout cap.
Priya: Can you paste a tiny snippet?
Alex: Sure — rough:

```go
// read SSE frames -> bodyBuf holds concatenated `data:` payloads
var bodyBuf bytes.Buffer
// append each `data:` line to bodyBuf
// when we observe empty line (event end) or size cap:
var item MyEvent
err := json.NewDecoder(bytes.NewReader(bodyBuf.Bytes())).Decode(&item)
if err == io.EOF || strings.Contains(err.Error(), "unexpected EOF") {
    // not a full object yet, wait for more chunks
} else if err != nil {
    // log and handle bad JSON
}
```

Alex: Also add a timer per partial event — if no completion within e.g. 2s, either drop or escalate depending on needed guarantees.
Jamal: For the Rust client, `serde_json::StreamDeserializer` or `Deserializer::from_slice` in streaming mode works but you must feed contiguous bytes for a full value. We keep a small Vec<u8> buffer, extend with each chunk, then call `StreamDeserializer::new(&buffer[..])` and iterate values while consuming bytes (or use the `consume()` hack). If the parser errors with EOF, wait for more bytes.
Mei: Quick Rust snippet (very rough):

```rust
use serde_json::Deserializer;
let mut buf = Vec::new();
// on each chunk: buf.extend_from_slice(&chunk);
let mut de = Deserializer::from_slice(&buf).into_iter::<MyEvent>();
match de.next() {
  Some(Ok(val)) => { /* got one, remove consumed bytes from buf */ }
  Some(Err(e)) => if e.is_eof() { /* wait */ } else { /* handle error */ }
  None => { /* need more */ }
}
```

Priya: Good. A few more questions: 1) how do we handle auth refresh/reconnect races where we reconnect and the server doesn't guarantee resume (partial event lost)? 2) any backpressure strategies to avoid lots of partials building up?
Alex: For auth: if you do seamless refresh and re-open the SSE connection, treat any partially-received event as non-resumable unless the protocol gives you an explicit resume token. So discard partial state on reconnect. If you care about exactly-once, you need server-side event ids + client ack.
Mei: Backpressure controls: implement a credit/window mechanism between reader goroutine and consumer goroutine. Reader reads from network into a bounded queue (channel with capacity N) and the consumer processes at its pace. If the channel is full, the reader should pause reads (or apply OS-level read buffer backpressure) — in practice you read into a small buffer then block on send to channel. Also consider slowing the server via request-level flow control if supported.
Jamal: Also add a hard buffer cap per-event (e.g. 64KB). If an event exceeds that before terminator, mark it as malformed and drop to avoid unbounded memory growth.
Priya: Ops/monitoring — what metrics should we add to catch this early?
Alex: partial_json_attempts, partial_json_timeouts, sse_reconnects, sse_bytes_buffered_gauge, sse_backpressure_pauses, json_decode_errors. Tag by route/model/client-version.
Mei: Add a devex test harness that emits intentionally split chunks to reproduce. Example curl-ish generator (server side simulate):

```bash
# crude netcat / bash example — send two chunks separated by a sleep
printf "event: message\ndata: {\"id\":1,\"text\":\"hi" > /tmp/stream1
sleep 0.3
printf " there\"}\n\n" >> /tmp/stream1
# then stream via nc to the client
cat /tmp/stream1 | nc -l 9000
```

Mei: Or a node script that writes `data:` frames with setTimeout between writes — easier to control.
Priya: For SDK change plan: 1) Buffer and only decode at event terminator, 2) Add 64KB cap + 2s per-event timeout, 3) On reconnect, drop partial unless resume token present, 4) Expose metrics and test harness. Any objections?
Jamal: LGTM. I'll prototype the Rust buffering/consume logic and add tests.
Alex: I'll send a small PR for the Go SDK with the bounded buffer + timeout and a unit test that simulates chunked delivery.
deploy-bot: Scheduled 2-nightly CI runs for devex/sse-buffer-fix branch. :robot_face:
Priya: Thanks all — I'll update the ticket with links to PRs and the reproduction script. If anyone finds an edge case (e.g., escaped newline inside string breaking naive newline split), call it out here.
Mei: One more edge: if server tries to compress multiple JSON objects into a single `data:` with embedded newlines, your concatenation must preserve original newlines between `data:` lines and not accidentally join separate events. Keep the event delimiter logic strict.
Priya: Good call. Ok closing this thread after PRs land. :thumbsup:
